Selection pressures on chains of thought are unavoidable
2026-11-18

A lot of noise has been made about how chain of thought monitoring represents a
substantial opportunity for observability of AI and interpretability. See [this
paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.11473) with dozens of high profile coauthors -
but there are countless such examples.

My claim is that pressures for the faithfulness and observability of reasoning
traces to disappear are structurally unavoidable. This post does not contain any
specific predictions about timelines, but a future post will - we can make
observations about the effects of these pressures and project those effects into
the future under several regulatory and economic regimes.

What this post is not:

* It is already well-known that reasoning traces are sensitive to explicit
  knowledge about whether the model is in an alignment eval. My argument is not
  about leaking knowledge of alignment evaluation criteria to models under
  evaluation - awareness is not necessary for selection pressure to operate.

* It is already well known that labs in a competitive environment are under
  pressure to abandon interpretability in favour of increased performance. There
  has been a lot of discussion about Astra's recurrent architecture (TODO:
  link). My argument is nothing to do with this. My claim is that even if labs
  do not apply any pressure on chains of thought and do not intentionally
  sacrifice chain of thought observability, it will still disappear over a long
  enough timeline.

Effort put into avoiding reinforcement learning pressure on chains of thought is
admirable and responsible, such pressure would be pernicious and likely to
result in credible yet unfaithful reasoning traces. But selection pressure is
inevitable and will only increase over time.

<!--more-->

It's worth examining the various _avoidable_ ways that reasoning traces will
stop being useful before examining the _unavoidable_ ways.

