## The central claim

So my claim is that:

* Without regulations forcing transparency to go away
* Without direct learning pressure
* Even if visible-space CoT is the most performant way to reason

CoTs will still become less faithful over time.

--- first draft below ---

Reasoning allows LLMs to construct complex, multi-step arguments and think using
what appears to be a human-like process. Thinking in English was at one point
trivially observable. Reasoning traces from GPT-o3, for example, contain actual
English reasoning that seems to show faithful thought processes that are not
constructed post-hoc.

Some interesting examples of such reasoning traces during alignment training can
be found in [this paper](https://www.antischeming.ai/).

It's easy to see why this observability might disappear. Either quickly,
    if chains of thought are subject to direct reinforcement learning and
    are part of grading. Or slowly, if indirect pressure operates over
    longer cycles. It is unlikely that anyone with the goal of reducing scheming
    would do anything as obviously counterproductive as training on reasoning
    traces. That could happen by accident (if the grader inadvertently gets access
            to traces), or intentionally, if the goal is to induce models to hide
    illegal reasoning to evade regulatory 

    But there is, in my view, literally no way at all to remove the
    pressures that will ultimately eliminate the faithfulness of
    chains of thought to the LLM's "real" thought process. There are a few
    mechanisms by which this might happen:

    1. Exposing chains of thought as part of alignment training. This is likely to
    result in
    This can be avoided by just...avoiding it. OpenAI has probably avoided it,
    DeepMind less so.

    2. Looking at and talking about chains of thought critically online. Talking
    about it in papers like [Apollo's paper on anti-scheming
    training](https://www.apolloresearch.ai/science/stress-testing-deliberative-alignment-for-anti-scheming-training)
    means that LLMs are more likely to know that exposing scheming in chains of
    thought results in non-deployment. This is a bit harder to avoid, but
    constructing environments that do not look like alignment evals is not the
    hardest problem. It is at least solvable.

    3. Then, there are the impossible to remove selection pressures. Is it already
    illegal for a business to use a model which contains open and obvious
    evidence of scheming and misalignment in its reasoning traces? As businesses
    switch to local AI (like [Revolut, which switched to local
            GLM](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/on-demand/session/gtcberlin26-sb61234/))
    and have broad access to reasoning traces - if those reasoning traces
    suggest scheming or thinking about illegal behaviour, will this hypothetical
    illegality make its way explicitly into law?  If it does, then the selection
    pressure on models will get cranked up to 11 - firms will be unlikely to
    deploy models that have misaligned thoughts, even if they do not act on
    them.

    As the capabilities of models to make larger leaps in reasoning without needing
    long chains of thought [see OpenAI's paper on Astra's CoT monitorability](https://alignment.openai.com/cot-monitorability-model-lifecycle/) increases, CoT decreases. This is also impossible to
    get around as long as capabilities increase.

    Let's review each of the mechanisms in a bit more depth and point to some
    reading you might want to do to get a sense of how bad things will get.

